# Trove — deployment configuration.
#
# This file lists what a DEPLOYMENT sets: secrets, endpoints, identity, and the
# handful of switches that decide which features are on. That is the whole
# contract, and tests/env-surface.test.ts keeps it that way.
#
# It deliberately no longer lists timeouts, intervals, retention windows,
# budgets or thresholds. Those were thirty variables that no deployment had ever
# set, and an unset variable is not configuration — it is a claim of
# configurability that nothing honours, paid for with a parse branch, a
# fallback, a test that cannot assume the default, and an unanswerable "what is
# this set to in production?". They are named constants next to the code they
# govern now, where changing one is a reviewable diff instead of an invisible
# edit in a dashboard.

# --- Topology -----------------------------------------------------------------
PORT=8787
DATABASE_URL=postgres://trove:trove@localhost:5433/trove
RAW_BLOB_BUCKET=trove-raw
# TROVE_STORE=postgres              # or "memory" for a throwaway in-process graph
# TROVE_AUTORUN_JOBS=1              # 0 to drain jobs manually via npm run jobs:run

# --- Identity and access ------------------------------------------------------
# Optional locally. Required in production.
# Format: token|actor_id|scope,scope;token2|actor_id2|scope
TROVE_SERVICE_TOKENS=local-dev-token|local-agent|graph:admin
# TROVE_SERVICE_OWNER_EMAIL=you@example.com   # account service tokens write as

# Clerk auth (hosted dashboard + per-user keys + OAuth connector). Optional
# locally; required for the claude.ai OAuth connector. See docs/oauth.md.
# CLERK_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_...
# CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_live_...
# VITE_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_live_...   # the same key, read at web build time
# TROVE_ADMIN_EMAILS=you@example.com

# --- Model providers ----------------------------------------------------------
# One resolver decides the endpoint for every LLM call — chat, reranking and the
# reconcile judge (src/llmProvider.ts). OPENROUTER_API_KEY wins when present:
# one key, many cheap models. OPENAI_API_KEY is the fallback, and OPENAI_BASE_URL
# names a specific gateway when you have one.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com/v1

# Embeddings do NOT go through that resolver and never should. The vectors
# already in the `embedding` column were produced by a specific model at a
# specific width; pointing this elsewhere does not switch providers, it corrupts
# a column. OPENAI_API_KEY, always.
# TROVE_EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=openai
# TROVE_EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
# TROVE_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=1536

# --- Features -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Graph chat — the dashboard's "ask the graph" panel (POST /v1/graph-chat).
# The traversal it streams IS a recall and needs no model: with this off the
# endpoint still streams every retrieval stage and returns the pack. The flag
# buys the prose, nothing else.
#
# Most models speak the classic body. A REASONING model instead wants
# max_completion_tokens + reasoning_effort and REJECTS temperature; either
# mismatch is a hard 400, so the shape is configured rather than guessed from
# the model name.
# TROVE_GRAPH_CHAT=1               # also accepts true/yes/on; anything else is off
# TROVE_CHAT_MODEL=meta/muse-spark-1.3-contributor
# TROVE_CHAT_REASONING_EFFORT=none # none for a classic chat model; minimal|low|medium|high for a reasoning one

# The next two are ON as soon as a provider key exists. They were opt-in, and
# both then sat dark for months in the only deployment that had a key — nothing
# failed, they simply never ran. An unset opt-in flag is indistinguishable from
# a deliberate no, so a feature behind one is only as live as somebody's memory.

# Read-time reranking of the recall candidate head. Fails open — a timeout or a
# bad reply just keeps the ordering recall had before. Measured on a real graph,
# the ordering is the bottleneck (Hit@K 100%, precision 23.3%), which is exactly
# what was dormant.
# TROVE_RECALL_RERANK=0            # off; anything else leaves it on
# TROVE_RECALL_RERANK_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o-mini

# Write-time reconciliation runs on every write, and its LLM judge is the only
# thing that can ever conclude "this note supersedes that one" — with the judge
# off, reconciliation runs a conservative heuristic that flags near-identical
# titles and never mutates the graph. Cost is bounded by construction: a
# calibrated distance gate excuses far candidates without a call, survivors are
# judged in ONE batched call per write, and a per-owner hourly budget backstops it.
# TROVE_RECONCILE_JUDGE=0          # off; anything else leaves it on
# TROVE_RECONCILE_JUDGE_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o-mini

# --- Observability ---------------------------------------------------------
# Langfuse tracing. Absent, tracing is off and costs nothing: no exporter, no
# OpenTelemetry import, no timer. Present, every LLM call in the product — the
# chat answer, the recall reranker, the reconcile judge, embeddings — is traced
# under the unit of work that asked for it (src/tracing.ts).
# LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=pk-lf-...
# LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=sk-lf-...
# LANGFUSE_BASE_URL=https://cloud.langfuse.com   # us.cloud.langfuse.com, or self-hosted
